 ],
 "persona": {
  "summary": "An empirical corporate finance economist whose signature contribution is confronting financial contracting theory with detailed contract-level and deal-level data. He is a leading authority on private equity, venture capital and bankruptcy, and one of the finance specialists on the prize committee.",
  "research_lens": "Views finance through incentive and contracting problems: agency, control rights, incomplete contracts and the institutions that address them (VC contracts, PE fund structures, bankruptcy procedures). Inference from his record: he would read candidate work in terms of whether theory maps onto real institutions and real contracts.",
  "methods_and_evidence": "Hand-collected proprietary micro data (VC term sheets and memos, buyout deal data, Swedish bankruptcy auctions), combined with targeted formal models. Typical outputs are careful descriptive-plus-test empirics and synthesis surveys.",
  "what_they_value_in_contributions": "Inferred from his work: theories that are testable and empirically validated; new data that reveals how institutions actually work; and contributions that bridge theory and practice in contract theory, corporate governance and financial intermediation. His own work builds directly on Hart, Holmström and Aghion–Bolton, which suggests he appreciates foundational contract theory and evidence on its real-world relevance.",
  "likely_questions_in_deliberation": [
   "Has the theory been confronted with real contracts or institutional data, and does it survive?",
   "Does the contribution change how we understand financing, governance or distress in actual firms and markets?",
   "How robust is the empirical identification, and is the data genuinely new or informative?",
   "Is the contribution foundational and broadly influential beyond one subfield, including in practice and policy?",
   "How does the work relate to the earlier prizes in finance and contract theory, and is it distinct enough from them?"
  ],
  "committee_experience": "Has served on the committee for multiple prize cycles, plausibly including the years of the 2013 (asset pricing), 2016 (contract theory) and 2022 (banking and financial crises) prizes. Finance-related scientific backgrounds would likely draw on his expertise, but I am not certain of his authorship of specific background documents."
 },
 "knowledge_limits": "My knowledge runs to roughly 2024. I am unsure of the exact year he joined the committee, his specific roles in particular prize years, and his authorship of scientific background documents. I have limited knowledge of his most recent papers (2021 onward) and their titles. I make no predictions about his 2026 views."
}

